  section "California Ballot Propositions"

    measure @ca-prop-2 "Proposition 2"
      subtitle "Public school facilities bond"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is a pretty straightforward bond for public school infrastructure in the
          state. California has a rather precarious funding structure for public schools
          because of 1978's Proposition 13 and the Gann Limit, which means that annual
          resource allocations are extremely volatile and depend primarily on the recency
          of real estate sales and their updated value appraisals, making bonds like this
          a necessary regular funding mechanism to ensure predictable funding, especially
          in years when the number of transactions are down and property values are not
          reassessed for tax purposes.

    measure @ca-prop-3 "Proposition 3"
      subtitle "Right to marry"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is essentially the opposite of 2008's Proposition 8, and enshrines the
          right to marry regardless of race, gender, sexual orientation, etc.

    measure @ca-prop-4 "Proposition 4"
      subtitle "Climate and wildfire bond"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          As wildfires become a nearly annual occurrence in the state, this bond will
          provide critical funding for necessary infrastructure to keep our communities
          safe from this growing threat.

    measure @ca-prop-5 "Proposition 5"
      subtitle "55% threshold for local housing bonds"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          State laws ensure that random asshats can jam up processes used by cities and
          counties to acquire affordable housing bonds. This law enables localities to set
          up these bonds as long as they have a 55% majority vote by the local electorate.
          Local housing bonds are good, and are generally more precise instruments than
          their state-wide alternatives.

    measure @ca-prop-6 "Proposition 6"
      subtitle "End involuntary servitude in prisons"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          Abolishes slavery. Slavery is bad.

    measure @ca-prop-32 "Proposition 32"
      subtitle "$18 minimum wage"
      recommend no-position
        justification:
          This law does two things: it sets up a ramped state-wide minimum wage to $18 by
          2026, and enables further increases based on inflation and cost of labor. Given
          that we're just coming off the backs of an inflationary macroeconomic
          environment, I won't begrudge anyone for voting no on this one, and I think that
          in general minimum wage laws are a good but limited policy lever for
          strengthening overall wages. Sectoral bargaining is better. That said, I
          personally will be voting "yes" on this one.

    measure @ca-prop-33 "Proposition 33"
      subtitle "Repeal Costa-Hawkins"
      recommend no
        justification:
          Costa-Hawkins is a loose, state-wide rent-control measure that caps increases at
          fixed percentages or inflation, and bans localities from instituting their own
          rent-control regimes unless they were already established before Costa-Hawkins.
          Prop 33 eliminates Costa-Hawkins, and enables local communities to enable their
          own rent control laws. Look, I'd love to get rid of Costa-Hawkins. It sucks. But
          the mechanism by which this law does this is pretty insane, and sets up
          literally zero limits on what localities can do in place of Costa-Hawkins. This
          comes at a time when cities all over the state are fighting back against the
          state government for enforcing much needed housing regulations. Prop 33 enables
          these notoriously anti-housing cities to effectively avoid much of the recent
          pro-housing legislation like AB2011 (CEQA reform), AB2097 (eliminating parking
          minimums next to transit centers), AB1551 (density bonus), and AB2234 (project
          timeline enforcement) by designing rent control schemes that make compliance
          with these laws impossible. Some cities have gone as far as to say this out
          loud. Most California cities have proven themselves to be untrustworthy when it
          comes to self-regulating their own housing production, and have fought tooth and
          nail to ensure that they can avoid state housing regulation. We need to get rid
          of Costa-Hawkins eventually, but we cannot do it at the expense of badly needed
          housing production in the state with the worst housing shortage in America.
          Vote No.

    measure @ca-prop-34 "Proposition 34"
      subtitle "Restrict spending of prescription drug revenue"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          NGOs in California have often received very little oversight over the public
          money that they have been deputized to spend. This law is a step in the right
          direction and dictates that 98% of the public health money they receive
          actually needs to be spent on the sanctioned care and not other infrastructure.
          The ballot measure is opposed only by one of the most notoriously negligent NGOs
          in the state, the AIDS Healthcare Foundation, which for some reason has dipped
          its toes into housing advocacy in the last decade. In general, anytime you see
          something endorsed by the AHF, you can pretty safely assume that you should
          oppose it.

    measure @ca-prop-35 "Proposition 35"
      subtitle "Permanent managed care tax for Medi-Cal"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This law establishes a permanent tax on managed care organizations (MCOs) to
          support Medi-Cal, California's Medicaid program that provides health coverage
          for low-income individuals. Major MCOs like Kaiser Permanente, Anthem Blue
          Cross, and Blue Shield deliver healthcare services through Medi-Cal.

          Revenue from the tax will be used to cover Medi-Cal enrollment and
          administrative expenses immediately. In 2025 and 2026, the tax is expected to
          generate $2.7 billion for Medi-Cal and health workforce initiatives, with an
          additional $2 billion directed to the general fund to help offset Medi-Cal
          costs. Starting in 2027, the tax will also help subsidize drug prices, and
          replaces the existing, annual, highly political process of funding Medi-Cal. It
          holds the Governor's mansion to the terms of the original MCO tax.

    measure @ca-prop-36 "Proposition 36"
      subtitle "Felony charges for certain drug and theft crimes"
      recommend no
        justification:
          This law essentially enables larger jail sentences for certain kinds of
          drug-related offenses by making them felonies instead of misdemeanors. While I
          am sympathetic to the desire to enforce basic order in public spaces (I live in
          San Francisco after all), we know from study after study that sentence length
          deterrence is not an effective mechanism to reduce crime. The basic theory is
          that when someone has decided to break the law, the difference between a 3 year
          and 5 year sentence has effectively zero effect on their decision. Each
          additional year of jail time establishes less and less deterrence, but has a
          linear impact on taxpayer spending, and an exponential negative impact on
          rehabilitation and recidivism. It's just not a good system. To effectively
          combat petty street crime (most of which actually isn't even covered by this
          law), cities need to actually invest in crime prevention, detectives, and
          performance management of their local police departments. This law does none
          of those.

