        support @aliya-chisti @ruth-ferguson @heather-mccarty @luis-zamora

  section "San Francisco Ballot Measures"

    measure @sf-prop-a "Proposition A"
      subtitle "School improvement bond"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This is quite similar to the state-wide Proposition 2, but is specific for San
          Francisco public schools, which typically face cost challenges of their own due
          to the high costs of the city.

    measure @sf-prop-b "Proposition B"
      subtitle "Health care, shelter, and street safety bond"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          San Francisco's public hospitals and public spaces could use the funding. We
          have extremely limited staff for street and sidewalk repair, and our public
          hospitals are in major need of renovation.

    measure @sf-prop-c "Proposition C"
      subtitle "Inspector general"
      recommend no-position
        justification:
          This ballot proposal creates an inspector general position in the controller's
          office. Such a position would be extremely redundant with the existing office of
          the City Attorney, the office of the District Attorney, the Sheriff's Office,
          and the Ethics Commission. Additionally, this new role does not bestow any new
          functional powers to the IG or any of the existing regulatory bodies, and adds
          at best an additional layer for coordination. You can vote for it if you want,
          but it doesn't really do anything significant and is mostly just blustering
          from Aaron Peskin. I personally will be voting no.

    measure @sf-prop-d "Proposition D"
      subtitle "City commissions and mayoral authority"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          San Francisco's insane public commission system is home to more than a hundred
          different public commissions staffed by well over a thousand commissioners.
          Commissioners are not elected officials. They're appointed, often politically,
          and cannot be effectively managed by the mayor's office or even the board of
          supervisors. This ballot proposition fundamentally retools the public
          commission system, instates a hard limit of 65 commissions, and adds
          considerable accountability to the functioning of these bodies by making them
          easier to regulate. The mayor and the board of supervisors should be the ones
          doing this stuff (and they're subject to the best form of public
          accountability: elections!)

    measure @sf-prop-e "Proposition E"
      subtitle "Commission streamlining task force"
      note:
        Proposition D and Proposition E are mutually exclusive measures. In the event
        that both of them cross the 50% threshold, the proposition with more votes will
        become law, and the other will be discarded.
      recommend no
        justification:
          Proposition E is a competing measure to Proposition D, and like Proposition C
          was placed on the ballot by mayoral candidate Supervisor Aaron Peskin.
          Proposition E attempts to reform the public commission system, but does so
          instead by forming a "Task Force" to study the various commissions and present
          recommendations to the public. It is a feckless regulation that does absolutely
          nothing. The "task force" is accountable to nobody, and there is no legal
          mechanism that ensures that what, if any, recommendations it produces are
          actually implemented.

    measure @sf-prop-f "Proposition F"
      subtitle "Deferred police retirement"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          San Francisco is facing a well documented police staffing shortage, and this
          proposal is a novel if temporary way to expand staffing by enabling police
          officers to defer retirement in exchange for additional overtime pay. I do not
          expect this law to make a significant dent in the staffing shortage, or to fix
          the numerous problems with police performance in San Francisco, but it's better
          than nothing.

    measure @sf-prop-g "Proposition G"
      subtitle "Rental subsidies for affordable housing"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          The existing low-income housing subsidies are not nearly enough, and this
          modest increase will provide more people with more access to housing in the
          city. Like Proposition F, I do not expect this law to make any serious
          improvements to housing subsidies or effectively combat the high costs of
          housing in San Francisco in general, but it's a relatively cheap fix that is
          better than doing nothing.

    measure @sf-prop-h "Proposition H"
      subtitle "Firefighter retirement age"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This brings the retirement age for San Francisco's firefighters back to 55,
          the age it was in 2012. Firefighters have difficult jobs with elevated health
          risks such as cancer, and we're not facing nearly the staffing shortage here as
          we are with the police.

    measure @sf-prop-i "Proposition I"
      subtitle "Retirement benefits for nurses and 911 dispatchers"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          Gives 911 dispatchers, who do incredible work, access to retirement benefits.
          In general we need far more of these people and we should make their jobs as
          attractive as we can make them. This law does other good things for nurses and
          other public health workers as well, but the retirement benefits for 911
          dispatchers is why you should vote yes.

    measure @sf-prop-j "Proposition J"
      subtitle "Children and youth fund consolidation"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          San Francisco currently houses six different youth related activity
          departments, in addition to the San Francisco Unified School District. Funding
          for these things is spread needlessly thin and has tons of extra cost built in
          because of the redundancy. This law unifies these systems into a single
          council, which should reduce costs, improve quality of service, and increase
          oversight.

    measure @sf-prop-k "Proposition K"
      subtitle "Close the Upper Great Highway to cars"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          Bans cars on the Great Highway. Cars are bad.

    measure @sf-prop-l "Proposition L"
      subtitle "Ride-hail tax for Muni"
      recommend no-position
        justification:
          Look, I'm an Uber employee and cannot in good faith tell you that my
          explanation for this isn't at least partially self interested, but I will just
          say that gross receipt taxes in general are not a great funding system, and
          this nominal tax increase will barely put a dent in Muni's funding cliff. To
          properly fix Muni's funding problem, you need a massive investment like 2022's
          Proposition A which unfortunately did not pass. This measure seems pretty
          obviously motivated by populist rage at self-driving cars, and it will not
          actually lead to significant changes to ride-sharing consumption or Muni's
          budget crisis. It will make things cost more, though.

    measure @sf-prop-m "Proposition M"
      subtitle "Business tax restructuring"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          San Francisco's increased office vacancy means the existing focus on low
          payroll taxes doesn't really work for us anymore. This system expands the
          availability of gross receipts tax exemptions to a larger number of businesses
          (annual sales of $5M instead of the previous $2.1M) and ensures that companies
          with a high number of remote workers are still paying their share. This
          restructuring is a necessary and fair reflection of the realities of the city's
          current budget and the new post-covid environment in which we can no longer
          reliably count exclusively on massive downtown employers for revenue at scale.

    measure @sf-prop-n "Proposition N"
      subtitle "First responder student loan forgiveness"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          This proposal is a novel way of forgiving student loans for first responders
          who work in the city and does so without an additional penny in extra public
          spending. EMTs, 911 dispatchers, and many others do critical work in our city,
          and we have critical staffing shortages for all of them. I don't think this law
          will actually do much in the way of addressing that shortage, but it's better
          than nothing and could help on the margin. It might also tell us whether such
          an approach could work at scale.

    measure @sf-prop-o "Proposition O"
      subtitle "Reproductive freedom"
      recommend yes
        justification:
          Enshrines the legal right to reproductive freedom in the city's health code.
          Reproductive freedom is good. The government telling you what to do with your
          body is bad.

